<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.4.1: Introducing the Zero Product Property</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02141</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.4.1: Introducing the Zero Product Property</md:title>
    <md:uuid>bf6444ae-c213-4583-9455-b509b0e5388d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>What values of the variables make each equation true?</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Solution" data-content-id="5b3c7463-9db4-4bf5-a56d-2da0188aa0dc" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="8ad43010-4e4f-445c-9428-d077659343f1" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-3">
    <problem>
      <para>\(6+2a=0\)</para>
      <para>Enter the value of a.</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(– 3\)</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="5a986493-88af-432e-8330-00139cfbe17e" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="0">
    <problem>
      <para>\(7b=0\)</para>
      <para>Enter the value of b.</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(0\)</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="81cbe54c-941e-4369-a118-64ef330c2b6c" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="3">
    <problem>
      <para>\(7(c−5)=0\)</para>
      <para>Enter the value of c.</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(3\)</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="3c031f12-8a5b-4bc4-b1f4-bffffb2e1cd5" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\(g=0\\). In other words, g equals zero and h may be any real number.&quot;, &quot;\\(h=0\\). In other words, h equals zero and g may be any real number.&quot;, &quot;\\(g=h=0\\). In other words, both g and h are equal to zero.&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(g=0\\). In other words, g equals zero and h may be any real number.&quot;, &quot;\\(h=0\\). In other words, h equals zero and g may be any real number.&quot;, &quot;\\(g=h=0\\). In other words, both g and h are equal to zero.&quot;, &quot;\\(g \\neq 0\\), \\(h \\neq 0\\). In other words, neither g nor h are equal to zero.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Determine which of the statements are true if \(g \cdot h=0\). HINT: There may be more than one statement that is true. </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The answer is (g=0\), \(h=0\), and \(g=h=0\). Either \(g=0\) or \(h=0\). But also, notice, if \(g=0\) and \(h=0\) at the same time, you can also say that \(g = h = 0\).</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> 
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE --> 
</div>
</div>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What did you notice about one of the factors in each of the previous problems?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter what you noticed.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> At least one of the factors in each equation was 0 or equivalent to 0.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>We will use the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">zero product property</term> to help solve <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">quadratic equations</term> throughout this lesson.</para>

<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold"> ZERO PRODUCT PROPERTY</para>
  <para>If the product of two expressions is 0, then one or both of the expressions equal 0.</para>
  <para>If \(ab=0\), then \(a=0\) or \(b=0\), or \(a=b=0\).</para>
</note></section>
</content>
